WEBVTT

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:05.720
Picture the same agent,

00:00:05.720 --> 00:00:07.030
on the same afternoon,

00:00:07.060 --> 00:00:08.270
with the same model.

00:00:08.340 --> 00:00:09.700
In one project,

00:00:09.700 --> 00:00:11.280
it writes code worthy of a senior

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:11.960
hire.

00:00:11.960 --> 00:00:12.980
In another,

00:00:12.980 --> 00:00:15.550
it produces something nobody wants to maintain.

00:00:15.580 --> 00:00:17.310
The difference isn't the prompt.

00:00:17.320 --> 00:00:18.630
It's the code base.

00:00:19.381 --> 00:00:20.611
In the first project,

00:00:20.621 --> 00:00:22.481
every endpoint follows one pattern:

00:00:22.481 --> 00:00:23.321
a handler,

00:00:23.321 --> 00:00:24.781
a schema and a test,

00:00:24.781 --> 00:00:25.961
named the same way.

00:00:25.961 --> 00:00:28.181
The agent reads the existing endpoints,

00:00:28.181 --> 00:00:29.281
follows the pattern,

00:00:29.281 --> 00:00:31.011
and the review takes three minutes.

00:00:31.101 --> 00:00:32.191
In the second,

00:00:32.221 --> 00:00:34.171
routes live in three different folders,

00:00:34.181 --> 00:00:35.631
and errors are thrown,

00:00:35.641 --> 00:00:36.441
returned,

00:00:36.441 --> 00:00:38.241
or quietly turned into null.

00:00:38.241 --> 00:00:40.581
The agent copies whatever it saw last,

00:00:40.581 --> 00:00:42.541
and the review takes thirty minutes of:

00:00:42.541 --> 00:00:44.191
that's not how we do it here.

00:00:44.967 --> 00:00:47.067
Convention is implicit context.

00:00:47.067 --> 00:00:49.107
An agent explores a codebase the way

00:00:49.107 --> 00:00:50.247
a new hire does.

00:00:50.247 --> 00:00:51.547
It looks for patterns.

00:00:51.547 --> 00:00:53.107
When tests always sit next to the

00:00:53.107 --> 00:00:54.207
file they test,

00:00:54.207 --> 00:00:55.767
nobody has to say where the new

00:00:55.767 --> 00:00:56.657
test goes.

00:00:56.747 --> 00:00:59.027
And consistent names make the agent's own

00:00:59.027 --> 00:00:59.887
tools work.

00:00:59.887 --> 00:01:01.727
When every test file ends the same

00:01:01.727 --> 00:01:02.127
way,

00:01:02.127 --> 00:01:04.097
a single search finds them all.

00:01:04.778 --> 00:01:07.078
The strongest convention is the instruction file

00:01:07.078 --> 00:01:08.728
at the root of the project.

00:01:08.818 --> 00:01:11.118
Agents .md has become the cross -tool

00:01:11.118 --> 00:01:11.798
standard.

00:01:11.798 --> 00:01:13.598
A good one has five parts.

00:01:13.598 --> 00:01:15.158
A project overview,

00:01:15.158 --> 00:01:17.068
the exact build and test commands,

00:01:17.178 --> 00:01:18.508
the architecture decisions,

00:01:18.718 --> 00:01:19.628
your conventions,

00:01:19.678 --> 00:01:21.048
and the common pitfalls.

00:01:21.118 --> 00:01:23.118
It takes about 30 minutes to write,

00:01:23.118 --> 00:01:25.298
and it gives every session more context

00:01:25.298 --> 00:01:26.858
than most people get in their first

00:01:26.858 --> 00:01:27.708
week.

00:01:28.135 --> 00:01:29.015
Keep it short.

00:01:29.015 --> 00:01:30.895
It's loaded on every session,

00:01:30.895 --> 00:01:32.475
so every line is a tax on

00:01:32.475 --> 00:01:33.545
every task.

00:01:33.615 --> 00:01:36.115
Point to deeper documents instead of pasting

00:01:36.115 --> 00:01:36.695
them in.

00:01:36.695 --> 00:01:37.835
And keep it up to date,

00:01:37.835 --> 00:01:40.335
because an outdated instruction file is worse

00:01:40.335 --> 00:01:41.015
than none.

00:01:41.015 --> 00:01:42.525
The agent will trust it.

00:01:43.183 --> 00:01:44.643
Procedures become skills:

00:01:44.643 --> 00:01:46.873
a folder with a skill file inside.

00:01:46.983 --> 00:01:49.123
The agent sees only each skill's name

00:01:49.123 --> 00:01:49.683
and description,

00:01:49.683 --> 00:01:51.673
until a task needs the rest.

00:01:51.723 --> 00:01:53.343
The progression is simple.

00:01:53.343 --> 00:01:55.423
A convention the agent keeps getting wrong

00:01:55.423 --> 00:01:57.043
goes in the instruction file.

00:01:57.043 --> 00:01:58.803
A procedure becomes a skill.

00:01:58.803 --> 00:02:00.523
A rule that must never be broken

00:02:00.523 --> 00:02:01.573
becomes a hook.

00:02:02.294 --> 00:02:04.534
Conventions are the closest thing agents have

00:02:04.534 --> 00:02:05.834
to long -term memory.

00:02:05.834 --> 00:02:06.814
Without them,

00:02:06.814 --> 00:02:08.694
you correct the same mistake in Session

00:02:08.694 --> 00:02:09.214
1,

00:02:09.214 --> 00:02:10.094
Session 2,

00:02:10.094 --> 00:02:11.364
and Session 3.

00:02:11.374 --> 00:02:12.534
Encode it once,

00:02:12.534 --> 00:02:14.514
in a documented pattern and a linter

00:02:14.514 --> 00:02:14.954
rule,

00:02:14.954 --> 00:02:16.284
and it's gone for good.

00:02:17.093 --> 00:02:19.153
Your codebase is the environment your agents

00:02:19.153 --> 00:02:19.813
live in.

00:02:19.813 --> 00:02:20.893
Make it legible.

00:02:20.893 --> 00:02:22.123
Make it predictable.

00:02:22.213 --> 00:02:23.363
Make it boring.

00:02:23.393 --> 00:02:25.173
The agents will repay you with code

00:02:25.173 --> 00:02:26.603
that looks like it belongs.

00:02:26.853 --> 00:02:27.813
Next up,

00:02:27.813 --> 00:02:28.843
the ship's log.
